.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt PDPCONTROL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pdpcontrol
.Nd control the PiDP-10 engine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm user | system
.Ar subcommand
.Op Ar boot
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
starts, stops and inspects the PDP-10 engine.
Systemd owns the process: with the default
.Cm user
scope
.Nm
drives the per-user
.Nm pidp10
units (the ones that need
.Ev DISPLAY
and PipeWire);
.Cm system
drives the system-scope unit
.Ar pidp10-engine@boot Ns No \&.service .
.Pp
Subcommands:
.Bl -tag -width restart
.It Cm start Op Ar boot
Boot a system.
.Ar boot
is the octal switch-register value the maintenance panel would be set to;
with no front panel attached you must give one (1 boots ITS, 2 TOPS-10 on
the stock selections file).
Which system directory a number boots is decided by
.Pa /usr/share/pidp10/systems/selections ;
with no
.Ar boot
the switch register decides, which sits at zero on a machine without a
front panel.
.It Cm stop
Polite stop: the simh
.Ic exit
command goes in through the console telnet port (via the
.Nm pidp10-stop
helper) so disk buffers are flushed, rather than the old
.Ic pkill -9 .
.It Cm restart Op Ar boot
Stop, then start.
.It Cm status
Which units are active.
.It Cm stat
Engine state through the console port (registers, halt/cont).
.It Cm console
Attach to the simh console prompt.
.It Cm halt
Halt the CPU without stopping the engine.
.El
.Pp
Operator policy is read from
.Pa /etc/pidp10/pidp10.conf
(the same file the units load), so
.Ev PIDP10_BOOT ,
.Ev PIDP10_PANEL
and
.Ev PIDP10_CONSOLE_PORT
set there apply here too.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic pdpcontrol start 1
Boot ITS on the user units.
.It Ic pdpcontrol system status
What the system-scope engine is doing.
.It Ic pdpcontrol stop
Stop whatever is running, flushing disks.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pdp 1 ,
.Xr systemctl 1 ,
.Pa /usr/share/doc/pidp10/README.md
